  "narrative": "During the 1930s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in tradition and identity. While specific documentation from this era is limited, it is clear that natural textures such as waves, curls, and kinks were prevalent among African cultures. Hairstyles like cornrows, which originated from traditional African braiding practices, were likely worn by women during this time. The afro, a style created by combing out natural growth of kinky hair, may have also been popularized in the 1930s as it is often associated with African American culture. Protective hairstyles, designed to minimize manipulation and exposure of Afro-textured hair to environmental elements, were likely employed to preserve the health and integrity of these beautiful textures. Unfortunately, without more specific information from this era, we can only speculate on the nuances of Black and African women's hairstyles during the 1930s, but it is evident that their hair culture was rich in tradition and identity.",
